  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.4. Increased capacity of Member States to improve social dialogue and labour relations laws, processes and institutions</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Reforms to the Law on Social Partnerships (#60 of 17.03.2023) and to the Law on Collective Bargaining (#128 of 28.12.2022) were passed by Parliament. The reforms benefited from ILO technical guidance, capacity building and legal reviews. ILO further facilitated consultations with social partners.
Further information:
https://cbd.minjust.gov.kg/1280?refId=1246113;
https://cbd.minjust.gov.kg/112457?refId=1206024
 - Issues Paper conducted, identifying key areas where legislative reform was required in 2018/2019. The Issues Paper was shared and discussed with tripartite constituents to identify the main directions of reform of the Labour Code
- A Concept was developed for the reform of the Labour Code, which included a proposed process for setting objectives, establishing a tripartite working group, and the steps and process for reform. The Concept was presented and discussed with tripartite constituents in November 2022 and guided the subsequent steps of the reform process.
- Draft provisions reviewed and commented in (1) the Law on Trade Unions; (2) the Labour Code; (3) the Law on Social Partnerships and (4) the Law on Collective Bargaining. A series of informal and formal comments were provided throughout 2022 (Law on Trade Unions) and in 2023 for the other legislation. 
- A Technical Memorandum on the draft Labour Code from the Labour Law Unit in Geneva is forthcoming (once complete it will also be shared and discussed with the tripartite constituents)
- A series of tripartite consultations and discussions on the draft LC was facilitated. 5 rounds of ILO comments disseminated comparative legal analyses and comparative legislation. 
- Capacity building support provided to each of the social partners to strengthen their understanding of ILS and to comment on the draft reforms.
- Capacity building workshops with the tripartite working group were held in late April 2023 in Bishkek to discuss and explain ILO comments to the draft Labour Code and relevant ILS and comparative legislation.
- Capacity building meetings with the Ministry of Labour in July and August 2023 to go through remaining ILO comments and strengthen understanding of ILS and comparative legislation. 
- Technical assistance to the drafting of new legislation on private employment agencies, based on an ILO gaps analysis on C181 (undertaken in 2022) and identified gaps in the national legislation.</narrative>
   </description>
